WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.085
Each cloud ships its own private DNS,
with its own console, API, zone model

2
00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:10.375
and failure behavior, and none was
designed to share policy with the others.

3
00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:15.951
AWS has Route 53 private hosted zones
answered by the VPC Resolver, inbound

4
00:00:15.951 --> 00:00:18.648
endpoints for queries from outside,

5
00:00:18.648 --> 00:00:22.875
outbound endpoints and Resolver
rules, and DNS Firewall.

6
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:27.284
Azure has Private DNS zones
resolvable only from linked networks,

7
00:00:27.284 --> 00:00:32.575
the Private Resolver's endpoints and
forwarding rulesets, and resolver policy.

8
00:00:32.575 --> 00:00:37.436
Google Cloud has private zones per
VPC network, inbound server policies,

9
00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:41.000
forwarding and peering zones,
and response policies.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.625
VMware NSX has no private zone at all:

11
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:48.350
a gateway forwarder holds a default
zone and up to five FQDN zones

12
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.900
of three servers each, used only
by workloads configured for it.

13
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.450
Azure private endpoints are the most
common source of private-IP split-brain.

14
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.894
With private DNS integration, a zone
group writes the endpoint's A record

15
00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:06.413
into the matching privatelink zone
and removes it when the endpoint goes.

16
00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:09.991
Public DNS carries a CNAME
into the privatelink name,

17
00:01:09.991 --> 00:01:12.350
so connection strings don't change.

18
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.525
The private answer comes back only from
a resolver that can see a linked zone.

19
00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:18.475
Three behaviors catch teams out.

20
00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:23.819
DNS answers don't grant access: the public
chain resolves from anywhere by design,

21
00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:26.690
and access is the
service's network setting,

22
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:31.475
so a wrong answer shows up as a
public-path connection or an access denial.

23
00:01:31.475 --> 00:01:36.259
Use one zone per service type everywhere,
with Microsoft's recommended names,

24
00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.950
since records are
generated only for those.

25
00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.779
And the NXDOMAIN trap: a partner's storage
account with its own private endpoint

26
00:01:43.779 --> 00:01:45.974
leads into your empty zone and fails;

27
00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:48.960
the fix is fallback to
internet on the zone's link.
